// MIT 6.828 (Fall 2012) — Lectures 13, 17, 18, 21, 22: overall OS
// organization (monolithic vs. microkernel vs. exokernel, and what
// virtualizing the CPU/memory actually buys an app), language/OS co-design
// via Microsoft's Singularity research OS (replacing hardware page
// protection with compile-time bytecode verification), scalable locking
// (why cache-coherent multicore hardware makes naive spinlocks collapse
// under contention, and the ticket/backoff/Anderson/MCS lock designs that
// fix it), lock-free coordination (cmpxchg-based data structures, the ABA
// problem, and RCU), and virtual machines (trap-and-emulate, shadow page
// tables, binary translation, and hardware VM support) — the course's
// final content module. See src/data/courses.ts for the full lecture map.
