    front: "List the five goals a kernel interface must balance, and state the central question this lecture organizes its whole survey (monolithic/microkernel/exokernel) around.",
    back: `**Five goals for a kernel**: **apps can use hardware resources** (the whole point of having a computer at all); **apps are easy and convenient to write** (a good abstraction saves application developers real work); **apps are multiplexed** — covering both **isolation** (apps can't hurt each other) and **sharing** (apps that want to cooperate can); **few kernel crashes** (the kernel itself must be robust, since everything depends on it); **the kernel can evolve** (new hardware, new features, new requirements over time, without a ground-up rewrite each time).

**The organizing question**: there are **lots of ways to structure an OS** — the lecture's real subject is **how to decide** among them, i.e. **what is the right kernel API?** — looking specifically for underlying **principles and approaches**, not just cataloguing historical designs. The rest of the lecture works through three genuinely different answers (traditional/monolithic, related card; microkernel; exokernel) to see what tradeoffs each one actually makes among the five goals above.`,
